sections.Empirische Prüfung und Gütekriterien:
Reliability: For the total score, internal consistencies (Cronbach's alpha) between a = .83 (Saile & Dieterich, 1992) and a = .90 (Luka-Krausgrill et al., 1994) were found.
Validity: To examine the unidimensionality of the PDI, principal component analyses with subsequent varimax rotation were computed separately for the four studies and for the total sample. Using the scree test as the criterion for the appropriate number of factors, one-factor solutions resulted in each case. In the total sample (n = 275 complete data sets), the factor explained 59 percent of the total variance (Dillmann et al., 1994).
The construct validity of the PDI is supported by moderate to high correlations with the following indicators of experienced disability:
"Down-Time", i.e. time spent by the patient resting or lying down during the day because of the pain (r = .40);
a self-developed "functional rating scale" for assessing specific behavioural impairments (r = .78);
Oswestry Low Back Pain Disability Questionnaire (OBQ; Fairbank, Couper, Davies & O'Brien, 1980; r = .76).
Criterion validity was examined via correlations with pain-related variables and measures of psychological impairment. Specifically, the PDI was found to be associated with - pain intensity (operationalized by visual analogue scales; r = .23 and r = .62, respectively);
the Beck Depression Inventory (BDI; Kammer, 1983; r = .52 and r = .26, respectively);
the depression scale CES-D (Hautzinger, 1988; r = .55).
Findings of a study of N = 284 inpatient pain patients (Wurmthaler et al., 1996), who were assigned to three chronification stages on the basis of the Mainzer Stadienkonzept chronischer Schmerzen, also support criterion validity; here, a tendency (p = .051) towards greater disability reported on the PDI with increasing degree of pain chronification was found.
Norms: Percentile ranks are available, which were obtained in four studies with a total of N = 309 pain patients (Dillmann et al., 1994). An analysis of variance revealed no statistically significant differences between the PDI sum scores of the studies; likewise, no age or sex effects were found.
